  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nRidging at upper levels with complimentary highs at 850mb and the \nsurface keep the weather picture consistent for the next \nseveral days. Southerly flow at the surface continues to push \nwarm air northward keeping temperatures above seasonal averages.\nNW flow aloft keeps those highs from getting to out of hand, \nbut high temperatures are expected to remain in the upper 70s \nto near 80 for most.\n\nThe upper level high remains stout across the central CONUS even \nmigrating a little northward over the next few days. This slows the \nmovements of a trough to the east and sets up the axis of flow west \nof our region keeping passing storm systems to the west and north. \nIt is not until Sunday before we expect some significant \nmovement in the upper level pattern. A trough across mid \nlatitudes of Canada indents the central CONUS high just enough \nto release the cut off low over the Rockies. This combined with\nanother system moving on shore across the Pacific NW sets up \nthe potential for expansive frontogenesis along a line from the\nnorthern Great Lakes through west Texas. As the Pacific NW low \nmakes its way further inland it ushers the previous stationary \nlow that over the Four Corners region eastward bringing with it chances\nfor showers and storms Monday evening moreso early Tuesday \nmorning. Probabilities for precipitation have been fairly low \nranging from 10-40 percent extending from the I-35 corridor to \npoints northwest. Models have been fairly conservative in \npropagating precipitation chances eastward. Even though \nmultiple days of warm air advection would make one think that \nthe atmosphere would be primed for convective activity, general \nsubsidence from a low to midlevel high across the eastern \nseaboard expanding westward yields a fairly stable environment \nahead of the low's passing. The question would be if the front \nis able to kinematically force ascent to develop showers and \nstorms. Pockets of ascent are likely as upper level CVA \ncompliments the low level kinematics of the cold front; \nhowever, there remains uncertainty how far south and east that \nenvironment propagates. If rain is able to develop and expand, \nPWAT vales around an inch present opportunities for isolated \nheavy downpours, but not as intense or expansive as we saw about\na month ago.\n\nLooking at the extended, these previously mentioned waves open up \nthe pattern fairly significantly. The general delineation \nbetween the warmer air across the southern CONUS and cooler air\nacross the north shifts southward opening up avenues for cooler\nCanadian air to beginning working its way into the region. This\nturns highs back down toward seasonal averages, yet still \nabove, around 65-70 degrees (average = 62). It also adds a bit \nmore turbulence to the pattern opening up chances for \nadditional showers and storms late next week particularly next \nSaturday; although, at this time scale there is a lot that can \nchange between now and then.",
